# loading — a path in, a set of declarations out.
#
# The largest impure edge in the system, and the one that hid longest because it
# runs before anything else exists. Booting a domain means asking the FILESYSTEM
# what is there : globbing a directory, testing whether a path is a directory,
# walking up a tree looking for a project's own ports, and evaluating each file
# it finds. All of it IO, none of it previously declared.
#
# Signal is REPLY : boot asks for a domain's declarations and gets them back.
#
# THE BOOTSTRAP. This port cannot be resolved the way the others are, because
# resolving it would mean reading the file that declares it — and reading files
# is the thing it does. So the loader keeps a deliberately minimal bootstrap :
# it reaches for the library's own ports and adapters directly, once, and
# everything after that goes through the bound adapter. That circularity is real
# and cannot be argued away ; what it CAN be is small, named, and confined to
# one method, rather than spread through boot as unexamined `Dir[]` calls.
#
# Declaring it anyway is the point. A port that exists only to describe an edge
# already taken is still worth having : it says what the edge IS, and it is what
# lets a second implementation — a gem that ships declarations, a manifest, a
# database of domains — be additive rather than a rewrite of boot.
#
# It declares NO fields : which paths to read is an argument to boot, not a
# per-deployment value.
Hecks.port "loading" do
  verb   "loaded_by"   # the how-verb a bind hangs off
  signal :reply        # returns declarations to boot
end
